      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To describe an interesting and instructive case of knee pain from nurse practitioner (NP) practice and discuss the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical evaluation, and treatment of osteosarcoma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DATA SOURCES",
          "content": "Findings from the history, physical examination, diagnostic testing, and follow-up of the case of an 18-year-old male who first presented with nontraumatic, diffuse left knee pain of 2.5-month duration."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "At follow-up, after a trial of conservative treatment, a lesion suspicious for osteosarcoma was seen in the proximal tibia. Osteosarcoma is a rare but dangerous cause of chronic extremity pain, especially in children and adolescents."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "IMPLICATIONS FOR PRACTICE",
          "content": "NPs must consider malignant bone tumors in the differential diagnosis of traumatic and nontraumatic extremity pain of extended duration, especially in children and adolescents. Thorough, persistent follow-up on recommended tests and referrals is necessary to ensure that important findings are not missed. The care of uninsured patients requires particular attention to cost concerns and access issues."
        }
      ]},
